WF-00 — Webhook Receiver

## Summary

- Inputs: Inbound WhatsApp webhook POST (Meta payload) at path `/whatsapp-astro-webhook`. After parsing (Step 2), WF-00 emits the canonical parsed-message payload it forwards downstream (WF-01 router, WF-60 logger):
  - `messageId` — string, **required**. Meta message id; the dedup key.
  - `timestamp` — string, **required**. Meta message timestamp (epoch seconds, as string).
  - `phoneNumber` — string, **required**. Sender MSISDN, digits only (no `+`).
  - `phoneNumberFormatted` — string, **required**. `+`-prefixed E.164 (`'+' + phoneNumber`).
  - `messageType` — string, **required**. `text` | `interactive` | `button` (template quick-reply tap, M5) | other (`image` / `audio` / …).
  - `messageContent` — string, **required**. Text body, or interactive id (`button_reply.id` / `list_reply.id` / `nfm_reply.response_json`), or for a template quick-reply tap the button label (`button.text`), or a `[TYPE]` bracket for other media.
  - `messageContentUpper` — string, **required**. `messageContent.toUpperCase().trim()` (downstream keyword matching).
  - `interactiveLabel` — string, *optional*. Display title for interactive button/list replies; `null` for `nfm_reply` and non-interactive types.
  - `contactName` — string, *optional*. WhatsApp profile name; `null` if absent.
  - `rawMessage` — object, **required**. The raw Meta `message` object (carried for audit / re-parse).
  - `metadata` — object, **required**. `{ phoneNumberId, displayPhoneNumber }` from `value.metadata`.
- Outputs: HTTP 200 response to Meta ({status: "processed"} or {status: "skipped"}); message ID recorded in Chinmay-Astro-Messages data table for dedup
- State Transitions: none
- Calls Sub-Workflows: WF-60 (inbound logging, direction='inbound'), WF-01 (Message Router)
- **Ambiguities / Notes:**
  - Sticky note in the workflow says "Message Cleanup >7 Days" but the actual dedup filter is `$today.minus(3,'days')`. Treat the 3-day window as truth.
  - The Meta webhook verification GET (`hub.challenge` echo) is not present in this workflow's connections — verify it lives elsewhere or is handled at the CF/n8n layer.
  - Bot-echo guard (live verified 2026-05-17): comparison is `message.from === value.metadata.display_phone_number.replace(/\D/g, '')` — strips non-digit characters from the WABA display phone and compares to the inbound sender. Meta does deliver outbound message echoes via the `messages` array in some scenarios; this guard is intentional (TD-030).
  - Whitespace-only guard (TD-034) applies to text messages only.
  - Interactive label extraction (TD-F, 2026-05-21): for `interactive` button_reply / list_reply, the parser captures BOTH the routable id (into `messageContent`, preserving WF-01 routing semantics) AND the display title (into `interactiveLabel`). `Build WF-60 Payload (Inbound)` writes `interactiveLabel || messageContent` into `messages.content` so the persisted audit row is human-readable; the id remains available downstream for routing.
  - Template quick-reply tap (M5; PDF-17 / PDF-19): a tap on a *template* quick-reply button arrives as `message.type='button'` with `message.button.{text,payload}` — a DIFFERENT webhook shape than an interactive `button_reply`. WF-00 logs it by its label (`button.text`) for a readable audit row and keeps `messageType='button'` on the forwarded payload. The conversion of the button label to the internal action id (`payment_completed` / `btn_feedback` / `btn_rebook` / `btn_done`) and the reshape into the interactive `button_reply` form happen POST-FILTER in WF-02 (after WF-01's country / blocked / opted-out filters), never here — so the conversion never runs for red-flagged-country or blocked traffic.

---

## Algorithm

Step 1: Start — receive HTTP POST on /whatsapp-astro-webhook from Meta WhatsApp Cloud API.
Step 2: Parse the webhook payload (extract body.entry[0].changes[0].value).
  - If payload has no entry, changes, or messages → mark skip=true, go to Step 3.
  - botPhone = value.metadata.display_phone_number with non-digit characters stripped (`replace(/\D/g, '')`). If message.from === botPhone (bot echo) → mark skip=true, go to Step 3. (TD-030)
  - If type='text' and content is empty/whitespace → mark skip=true, go to Step 3. (TD-034)
  - Otherwise extract: messageId, timestamp, phoneNumber, contactName, messageType, messageContent (text body, or for interactive: button_reply.id / list_reply.id / nfm_reply.response_json (cleartext form payload — Flow is in send-only mode, no encryption-svc needed); or for type='button' (template quick-reply tap, M5): button.text with button.payload as fallback), interactiveLabel (for interactive button_reply.title / list_reply.title, or for type='button' the button.text; null for nfm_reply and non-interactive types), metadata (phone_number_id, display_phone_number). Set skip=false. (A template-button tap keeps messageType='button' here; WF-02 normalizes it post-filter.)
Step 3: Check: skip == true?
  - If YES → go to Step 11 (return skipped).
  - If NO → go to Step 4.
Step 4: Lookup messageId in Chinmay-Astro-Messages data table (dedup check).
Step 5: Check: did the lookup return an existing row (duplicate)?
  - If YES (id is not empty) → go to Step 11 (return skipped — already processed).
  - If NO → go to Step 6.
Step 6: Insert new row into Chinmay-Astro-Messages with messageId (records this message as seen).
Step 7: Delete all rows in Chinmay-Astro-Messages where createdAt <= today - 3 days (housekeeping; sticky note labels "7 days" but actual filter is 3 days).
Step 8: Pass parsed message payload forward (Gather Message Info — passthrough of parsed fields).
Step 8a: Call WF-60 (Message Logger) with the canonical WF-60 input shape (data-contract-discipline Phase 1 — design.md §2.6 `transport='wa'` `direction='inbound'` variant):
  { transport:'wa', direction:'inbound', userId:null, consultationId:null, messageType, phoneNumber,
    whatsappMessageId: messageId,
    content: (messageType=='interactive' ? (interactiveLabel ?? messageContent) : messageContent),
    metadata: { phoneNumber, contactName, timestamp, rawMessage,
                ...(messageType=='interactive' && interactiveLabel ? { interactiveButtonId: messageContent } : {}) }
  }. The `transport` discriminator is the canonical Phase-1 marker — WF-60's entry guard requires it. WF-60 handles all inbound audit; failures here MUST NOT block routing — wrap in onError='continueRegularOutput'.
Step 9: Call WF-01 (Message Router) with the parsed message payload.
Step 10: Return HTTP 200 with {status: "processed"} to Meta.
Step 11: Return HTTP 200 with {status: "skipped"} to Meta.
Step 12: End.

Note: Meta GET verification (hub.challenge) is handled by a separate webhook or n8n-side test config — not present as a branch in this workflow's connections.
